(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "8.6", title: "Physical computing with micro:bit", q: "How can a program sense and change the real world?", lessons: [
{ t: "Embedded systems and the micro:bit", o: "I can explain what an embedded system is and identify the parts of a micro:bit.",
  k: ["embedded system: a computer built into a device to do one particular job", "microcontroller: a small computer on a single chip, with its own processor and memory", "micro:bit: a pocket-sized programmable computer with built-in sensors and lights"],
  d: ["Name three devices at home that contain a computer but are not called computers.", "For example a washing machine, microwave, TV remote, thermostat, car."],
  s: ["Embedded systems|A computer inside another device|It does one job, again and again|Washing machines, traffic lights, fitness trackers",
      "The micro:bit|25 LEDs in a 5 x 5 grid|Two buttons, A and B|Sensors: movement, light, temperature, compass|Radio, pins for connecting other parts, a USB socket",
      "How you program it|Write Python in the micro:bit editor|Send the program to the micro:bit over USB|It runs by itself, even on batteries"],
  a: "Label the parts of the micro:bit on the diagram, classify each as input, output or both, and run the demo program to see each feature. Then list five embedded systems and their inputs and outputs. Support: match the label cards to the board. Stretch: explain why a washing machine uses an embedded system instead of a full PC.",
  w: [["What is an embedded system?", "A computer built into a device to do one particular job."], ["How many LEDs are on the micro:bit's display?", "25."], ["Name three sensors built into the micro:bit.", "Any three: accelerometer (movement), light, temperature, compass."], ["Classify as input or output: button A, LED display, accelerometer.", "Input, output, input."], ["Explain one way an embedded system differs from a laptop.", "It runs one fixed program for one job, with only the hardware that job needs, so it is smaller and cheaper and uses less power."]],
  x: ["Give one embedded system and its job.", "For example a thermostat that controls heating."] },
{ t: "The display and images", o: "I can show text and images on the micro:bit display.",
  k: ["display: the 5 x 5 grid of LEDs", "scroll: to move text across the display", "sleep: to pause the program for a number of milliseconds"],
  d: ["How many milliseconds are in one second?", "1000."],
  s: ["First program|>> from microbit import *|>> display.scroll('Hello')|The first line gives access to the micro:bit's features",
      "Images|>> display.show(Image.HEART)|>> sleep(1000)|>> display.show(Image.HAPPY)|`sleep(1000)` waits one second",
      "Repeating|>> while True:|>>     display.show(Image.HEART)|>>     sleep(500)|>>     display.clear()|>>     sleep(500)"],
  a: "Write programs that scroll your name, show three built-in images in turn, and make a beating heart with a `while True` loop. Test in the simulator, then on the device. Support: change the text and images in the starter program. Stretch: light single LEDs with `display.set_pixel(x, y, 9)` to draw your own shape.",
  w: [["Which line must be at the top of every micro:bit program?", "from microbit import *"], ["What does `display.scroll('Hi')` do?", "It moves the text Hi across the display."], ["What does `sleep(2000)` do?", "It pauses the program for 2 seconds."], ["Why is `while True:` used in most micro:bit programs?", "So the program keeps running and repeating for as long as the device is on."], ["An animation flashes too fast to see. Explain the fix.", "Add or increase a sleep after each image so each one stays on long enough to be seen."]],
  x: ["How many milliseconds would you sleep for half a second?", "500."] },
{ t: "Lab: animated name badge", o: "I can build a program that combines text, images and a loop.",
  k: ["animation: images shown one after another to give the effect of movement", "frame: one image in an animation", "simulator: an on-screen micro:bit for testing programs"],
  d: ["What two things does an animation need between frames?", "A new image and a short pause."],
  s: ["Plan|Your name or initials|At least three frames of animation|It repeats for ever",
      "Build|Test in the simulator first|Send it to the micro:bit|Connect the battery pack and wear it",
      "Polish|Is each frame visible for long enough?|Is the text readable from a distance?|Does it restart cleanly?"],
  a: "Design and build an animated name badge: scrolling name, then an animation of at least three frames, repeating. Test in the simulator and on the device. Support: start from the badge template. Stretch: design your own frames using `Image('09090:99999:99999:09990:00900')`.",
  w: [["What is a frame?", "One image in an animation."], ["Why test in the simulator before using the device?", "It is quicker and shows errors before the program is sent to the micro:bit."], ["Which loop makes the badge repeat for ever?", "while True:"], ["Your name scrolls once and then the display stays blank. What is missing?", "The code is not inside a while True loop."], ["Explain how you chose the sleep time between frames.", "By testing: long enough to see each frame, short enough to look like movement."]],
  x: ["How many frames does your animation have?", "A number of at least 3."] },
{ t: "Buttons and events", o: "I can make a program respond to button presses.",
  k: ["event: something that happens that a program can respond to", "polling: checking again and again whether something has happened", "is_pressed: a test that is True while a button is held down"],
  d: ["In Scratch, which block ran a script when a key was pressed?", "when ... key pressed."],
  s: ["Checking a button|>> while True:|>>     if button_a.is_pressed():|>>         display.show(Image.HAPPY)|>>     else:|>>         display.clear()",
      "Two ways to test|`is_pressed()`: True while the button is held down now|`was_pressed()`: True if it has been pressed since you last asked|Use `was_pressed()` for counting presses",
      "More than one button|`if button_a.is_pressed() and button_b.is_pressed():` both together|Use `elif` for B alone, then A alone|Test both first, or it will never be reached"],
  a: "Write a program that shows a different image for button A, button B and both together, and nothing when neither is pressed. Support: complete the program with the A and B branches missing. Stretch: make button A scroll a question and button B scroll the answer.",
  w: [["What does `button_a.is_pressed()` give?", "True while button A is held down, otherwise False."], ["What is polling?", "Checking again and again whether something has happened."], ["Why must the button test be inside `while True:`?", "So it is checked repeatedly. Outside the loop it would be checked only once at the start."], ["Which should be used to count presses: `is_pressed()` or `was_pressed()`? Why?", "was_pressed(), because it counts each press once however long the button is held."], ["Explain why the test for both buttons must come before the tests for A alone and B alone.", "If A alone were tested first, it would be True whenever both are pressed, so the 'both' branch would never run."]],
  x: ["Name the two buttons on the micro:bit.", "A and B."] },
{ t: "Variables and counters", o: "I can use a variable to count events on the micro:bit.",
  k: ["counter: a variable that goes up by one each time something happens", "initialise: to set a variable's starting value", "reset: to set a variable back to its starting value"],
  d: ["Write the Python line that adds 1 to a variable called `count`.", "count = count + 1"],
  s: ["A press counter|>> count = 0|>> while True:|>>     if button_a.was_pressed():|>>         count = count + 1|>>     display.show(count)",
      "Reset|>>     if button_b.was_pressed():|>>         count = 0|Button B sets the counter back to zero",
      "Take care|Initialise before the loop, not inside it|`display.show` only shows one digit at a time: use `display.scroll(count)` for larger numbers|`str(count)` turns the number into text if you want to join it to words"],
  a: "Build a counter that goes up with button A, down with button B and resets when both are pressed. Then adapt it into a scoreboard for two teams. Support: add the 'down' button to the working 'up' counter. Stretch: stop the counter going below 0 or above 99.",
  w: [["What is a counter?", "A variable that goes up by one each time something happens."], ["Where must `count = 0` be written?", "Before the while True loop."], ["What happens if `count = 0` is inside the loop?", "The counter is reset to 0 every time round, so it never goes up."], ["Which display command should be used for the number 27?", "display.scroll(27)"], ["Explain how to stop the counter going below zero.", "Only subtract 1 if count is greater than 0: use an if test before changing it."]],
  x: ["Which button test counts each press exactly once?", "was_pressed()"] },
{ t: "Lab: step counter or reaction timer", o: "I can build a useful device that uses a counter or a timer.",
  k: ["gesture: a movement the micro:bit can recognise, such as a shake", "running time: the number of milliseconds since the program started", "reaction time: how long someone takes to respond"],
  d: ["A step counter needs to notice each step. Which sensor could detect a step?", "The accelerometer, which senses movement."],
  s: ["Step counter|>> if accelerometer.was_gesture('shake'):|>>     steps = steps + 1|Each shake counts as one step|Show the total when button A is pressed",
      "Reaction timer|Wait a random time, then show an image|Record `running_time()` when it appears and again when the button is pressed|The difference is the reaction time in milliseconds",
      "Test|Count 20 real steps: what does the device say?|Is it over-counting or under-counting?|Record three trials"],
  a: "Build either the step counter or the reaction timer. Test it with three trials, record the results and write one improvement. Support: complete the step counter from the part-written program. Stretch: build both and add a 'best score' that is remembered until reset.",
  w: [["Which sensor does the step counter use?", "The accelerometer."], ["What does `accelerometer.was_gesture('shake')` detect?", "Whether the micro:bit has been shaken since it was last checked."], ["What does `running_time()` give?", "The number of milliseconds since the program started."], ["You walk 20 steps and the counter shows 26. What does this tell you?", "It is over-counting: some movements are counted as steps more than once."], ["Explain how a reaction time is calculated from two readings of the running time.", "Subtract the time when the image appeared from the time when the button was pressed."]],
  x: ["What was your most accurate trial?", "A result from the student's table."] },
{ t: "Sensors: movement, light and temperature", o: "I can read values from the micro:bit's sensors.",
  k: ["accelerometer: a sensor that measures tilt and movement in three directions", "reading: one value taken from a sensor", "analogue: able to take any value in a range, not just on or off"],
  d: ["A button gives two values: pressed or not. How many values might a light sensor give?", "Many: a range from dark to bright."],
  s: ["Reading sensors|`temperature()` gives degrees Celsius|`display.read_light_level()` gives 0 (dark) to 255 (bright)|`accelerometer.get_x()` gives tilt left and right, roughly -1000 to 1000",
      "Showing a reading|>> while True:|>>     display.scroll(temperature())|>>     sleep(2000)",
      "A range of values|Buttons are digital: 0 or 1|Sensors are analogue: many values|The program decides what to do with the number"],
  a: "Write three short programs that display the temperature, the light level and the tilt. Record five readings from each in different conditions (covered, uncovered, tilted left and right). Support: run the three ready-made programs and record the readings. Stretch: show the tilt as a dot that moves across the display.",
  w: [["What does an accelerometer measure?", "Tilt and movement."], ["What range of values does the light level have?", "0 to 255."], ["What does `temperature()` give?", "The temperature in degrees Celsius."], ["Is a button digital or analogue? Is a light sensor?", "A button is digital. A light sensor is analogue."], ["Explain why a sensor reading is more useful to a program than a simple on or off.", "The program can respond to how much, for example how dark or how tilted, and can set its own level at which to act."]],
  x: ["What was the lowest light level you recorded, and how did you get it?", "A value near 0, by covering the display."] },
{ t: "Thresholds and noisy data", o: "I can use a threshold to turn a sensor reading into a decision.",
  k: ["threshold: the value at which the program changes what it does", "noise: small random changes in sensor readings", "calibrate: to adjust a threshold by testing in real conditions"],
  d: ["A street light should switch on when it gets dark. The light sensor gives 0 to 255. How does the program decide what counts as 'dark'?", "It compares the reading with a chosen number: a threshold."],
  s: ["Thresholds|>> if display.read_light_level() < 50:|>>     display.show(Image.SQUARE)|Below the threshold: lights on|The number 50 is a choice",
      "Noise|Readings wobble even when nothing changes|Near the threshold, the output can flicker on and off|Real data is never perfectly steady",
      "Choosing a threshold|Record readings in 'dark' and in 'light'|Pick a value clearly between them|Test it and adjust: this is calibration"],
  a: "Record ten light readings in the dark and ten in the light, find the range of each, and choose a threshold between them. Write a program that uses it, test it and adjust. Support: use the readings table provided and choose the threshold. Stretch: reduce flicker by using two thresholds, one for switching on and a higher one for switching off.",
  w: [["What is a threshold?", "The value at which the program changes what it does."], ["What is noise in sensor data?", "Small random changes in the readings."], ["Dark readings are 5 to 20 and light readings are 120 to 200. Suggest a threshold.", "Any value clearly between, such as 70."], ["Why might a light flicker when the reading is close to the threshold?", "Noise moves the reading above and below the threshold repeatedly."], ["Explain what calibration means and why it is needed.", "Adjusting the threshold by testing in the real conditions, because readings differ between rooms, devices and times of day."]],
  x: ["What threshold did you choose, and did you have to change it?", "A value and whether it was adjusted."] },
{ t: "Lab: night light and tilt alarm", o: "I can build devices that respond to sensor readings.",
  k: ["input-process-output: sensing, deciding, then acting", "alarm: an output that warns of a condition", "condition: a test that is True or False"],
  d: ["For a night light, what is the input, the process and the output?", "Input: the light level. Process: compare with the threshold. Output: LEDs on or off."],
  s: ["Night light|Read the light level|If it is below your threshold, light the display|Otherwise clear it",
      "Tilt alarm|Read `accelerometer.get_x()`|If it is beyond a threshold in either direction, show a warning|Use `or` to test both directions",
      "Test with real conditions|Cover and uncover the sensor|Tilt slowly: at what angle does it trigger?|Record the results"],
  a: "Build the night light and the tilt alarm, each with a calibrated threshold. Complete a test table for each with at least five tests. Support: build the night light from the part-written program. Stretch: combine them: the alarm only works when it is dark.",
  w: [["What is the input of the night light?", "The light level."], ["Write a condition that is True when the tilt reading `x` is more than 400 either way.", "x > 400 or x < -400"], ["What is the output of the tilt alarm?", "A warning image (or sound)."], ["Give one test for the night light and its expected result.", "For example cover the sensor: the display lights up."], ["Explain how to make the alarm work only in the dark.", "Combine the conditions with and: the tilt is past its threshold and the light level is below its threshold."]],
  x: ["Describe one of your systems as input, process, output.", "Three correct parts."] },
{ t: "Radio: sending messages between devices", o: "I can send and receive messages between micro:bits.",
  k: ["radio: wireless communication between micro:bits", "group: a channel number; only micro:bits in the same group hear each other", "protocol: an agreed set of rules for communicating"],
  d: ["Thirty micro:bits in one room all send messages at once. What problem might there be?", "Every device hears every message. They need separate channels."],
  s: ["Switching on|>> import radio|>> radio.on()|>> radio.config(group=7)|Both devices must use the same group number",
      "Sending and receiving|>> radio.send('hello')|>> message = radio.receive()|>> if message:|>>     display.scroll(message)|`receive()` gives None when nothing has arrived",
      "Agreeing the rules|What messages are allowed?|What does each one mean?|That agreement is a protocol, like HTTP in Unit 8.2"],
  a: "In pairs, with your own group number, write a program that sends 'A' or 'B' when a button is pressed and shows whatever is received. Then agree a protocol of three messages and what each means. Support: use the send and receive starter and set your group number. Stretch: send a number as text and convert it back with `int()` on the receiver.",
  w: [["Which two lines switch the radio on?", "import radio and radio.on()"], ["What is a radio group for?", "It is a channel number. Only micro:bits in the same group receive each other's messages."], ["What does `radio.receive()` give when no message has arrived?", "None."], ["Why is `if message:` needed before displaying it?", "To avoid trying to display nothing when no message has arrived."], ["Explain what a protocol is, using your messages as an example.", "An agreed set of rules. For example both devices agree that 'A' means yes and 'B' means no."]],
  x: ["What group number did your pair use, and why did it need to be different from other pairs?", "A number, so that other pairs' messages were not received."] },
{ t: "Outputs: sound and external LEDs", o: "I can control sound and an external LED from the micro:bit.",
  k: ["pin: a connection on the edge of the micro:bit for attaching other parts", "digital output: a signal that is either on (1) or off (0)", "circuit: a complete loop that electricity can flow around"],
  d: ["The micro:bit has only 25 small LEDs. How could it control something else, such as a bigger light or a buzzer?", "Connect it to the pins on the edge."],
  s: ["Sound|>> import music|>> music.play(music.BA_DING)|>> music.pitch(440, 500)|A note of 440 hertz for 500 milliseconds",
      "Pins|The large pads are pin 0, pin 1, pin 2, 3V and GND|`pin0.write_digital(1)` switches pin 0 on|`pin0.write_digital(0)` switches it off",
      "An external LED|Clip pin 0 to the long leg of the LED, through a resistor|Clip the short leg to GND|That completes the circuit"],
  a: "Play three built-in tunes, then write a program that plays a higher note for button A and a lower note for button B. Connect an external LED to pin 0 and make it blink. Support: follow the wiring diagram and run the blink program. Stretch: make the LED flash faster when the micro:bit is tilted further.",
  w: [["Which module is needed for sound?", "music"], ["What does `music.pitch(440, 500)` do?", "Plays a note of 440 hertz for 500 milliseconds."], ["What does `pin0.write_digital(1)` do?", "Switches pin 0 on."], ["Which pin does the short leg of the LED connect to?", "GND."], ["The LED does not light. Give two things to check.", "Any two: the LED is the right way round, the clips are on the correct pins, the circuit is complete, the program sets the pin to 1."]],
  x: ["Is switching an LED on and off a digital or an analogue output?", "Digital."] },
{ t: "Lab: two-device messaging game", o: "I can build a program in which two devices work together.",
  k: ["sender: the device that transmits a message", "receiver: the device that gets the message", "synchronise: to make two devices act together"],
  d: ["In a game of rock, paper, scissors over radio, what must both players' programs agree on?", "The group number and what message stands for each choice."],
  s: ["Choose a game|Rock, paper, scissors: shake to choose, send the choice, show the result|Or a quiz buzzer: the first to press wins|Or pass the dot: tilt to send a dot to the other display",
      "Plan the protocol|List every message|Say what each device does when it receives it|Write it down before coding",
      "Build and test|Both partners load the same program|Test each message one at a time|Record what worked and what did not"],
  a: "In pairs, plan the protocol for one game, then build and test it on two micro:bits. Record a test for each message. Support: build the quiz buzzer from the starter. Stretch: add a score that both devices keep in step.",
  w: [["Which game did you build?", "A named game."], ["List the messages in your protocol.", "A list of messages with meanings."], ["What does the receiver do when it gets your first message?", "A described action."], ["Describe one problem you met when the two devices communicated.", "A specific problem."], ["Explain why both devices need exactly the same protocol.", "If they disagree about what a message means, the receiver does the wrong thing or ignores it."]],
  x: ["Did both devices always agree on the result? If not, why?", "An observation."] },
{ t: "Design brief: a device that helps someone", o: "I can analyse a brief and generate ideas for a product.",
  k: ["design brief: a short description of what a product must do and for whom", "user: the person who will use the product", "requirement: something the product must do"],
  d: ["Think of someone you know. What small daily problem could a simple device help with?", "For example forgetting to water plants, leaving a door open, needing a reminder."],
  s: ["The brief|Design a micro:bit device that helps a particular person|It must use at least one sensor or button and one output|It must make a decision",
      "Ideas|Plant moisture reminder, posture alarm, fridge-door alert|Step goal tracker, quiet-classroom meter, medicine reminder|Start with the user's problem, not with the technology",
      "Choosing|Who is the user and what is the problem?|Can it be built in two lessons?|Which inputs and outputs does it need?"],
  a: "In teams of three or four, brainstorm at least six ideas, score each against the brief and choose one. Write a problem statement: user, problem, and how the device helps. Support: choose from the idea cards. Stretch: interview a real potential user and record their requirements.",
  w: [["Who is your user?", "A particular person or group."], ["What problem will your device help with?", "A clear problem."], ["Which inputs will it use?", "A sensor or button."], ["Which outputs will it use?", "Display, sound or an external part."], ["What decision will the device make? Write it as 'if ... then ...'.", "A rule with a condition and an action."]],
  x: ["Write your problem statement in one sentence.", "User, problem and solution in one sentence."] },
{ t: "Team roles, plan and success criteria", o: "I can plan a product as a team.",
  k: ["role: a person's job in a team", "flowchart: a diagram of the steps and decisions of a program", "success criteria: what the finished product must do"],
  d: ["What could go wrong if everyone in a team tries to write the program at once?", "Work is duplicated, changes clash, and nobody tests or records."],
  s: ["Roles|Programmer: writes the code|Tester: plans and runs tests|Designer: builds the casing and wiring|Presenter: records progress and leads the demonstration",
      "The plan|Describe the device as input, process and output|Draw a flowchart of the program|Sketch the product and how it is worn or placed",
      "Success criteria|Five 'The device must...' statements|Each one must be testable|They become your test table"],
  a: "Agree roles, then complete the team planning sheet: input-process-output table, flowchart, sketch and five success criteria. Support: use the planning frame with prompts. Stretch: plan a second feature to add if time allows.",
  w: [["What is your role, and what are you responsible for?", "A role and its responsibilities."], ["Describe your device as input, process and output.", "Three parts."], ["Write the main decision in your flowchart.", "A condition."], ["Write two of your success criteria.", "Two testable statements."], ["Explain why success criteria must be testable.", "So the team can prove with a test whether each one has been met."]],
  x: ["What will your team do first next lesson?", "A specific first task."] },
{ t: "Lab: build sprint 1", o: "I can build and test the core of my team's product.",
  k: ["core: the most important feature", "prototype: an early working version", "log: a record of what was done and found"],
  d: ["What is the core feature of your device, the one thing it must do?", "The main input leading to the main output."],
  s: ["Core first|Read the input|Make the decision|Produce the output|Leave decoration and extras for later",
      "Work as a team|Programmer codes while the tester prepares the test table|Designer builds the casing|Presenter keeps the log",
      "Test early|Try it in real conditions|Calibrate any thresholds|Record every test"],
  a: "Build a prototype of the core feature, calibrate it and run at least three tests. Keep the team log up to date. Support: start from the closest example program. Stretch: begin the second feature.",
  w: [["What did your team build today?", "A description of the prototype."], ["What did you do in your role?", "Role-specific work."], ["Record one test: what you did, what you expected, what happened.", "A complete test."], ["What threshold or setting did you have to adjust?", "A value and the change."], ["What is the most important thing to do next lesson?", "A priority task."]],
  x: ["Which success criteria does the prototype already meet?", "A list."] },
{ t: "Build sprint 2 and testing", o: "I can complete my product and test it against its success criteria.",
  k: ["test table: a record of tests with expected and actual results", "reliable: working correctly every time", "fix: a change that corrects a fault"],
  d: ["A device works once on your desk. Is that enough to call it reliable?", "No. It must work repeatedly and in the conditions where it will be used."],
  s: ["Finish|Complete the remaining features|Fit the device into its casing|Check the battery pack and connections",
      "Test against the criteria|One or more tests for each success criterion|Record expected and actual results|Repeat important tests three times",
      "Fix and retest|Fix failures in order of importance|Retest after every fix|Note what still does not work"],
  a: "Complete the product and fill in the test table with at least five tests covering every success criterion. Fix and retest failures. Support: the tester works with the teacher's test table template. Stretch: ask someone outside the team to use the device without instructions.",
  w: [["How many of your success criteria are met?", "A number out of 5."], ["Record a test that failed, and the fix.", "A failed test and its fix."], ["Why repeat a test three times?", "To check the device is reliable and the result was not chance."], ["What still does not work as planned?", "An honest answer."], ["Explain how the test table proves that your product works.", "Each criterion has a test with an expected and an actual result that match."]],
  x: ["Which part of the product are you most pleased with?", "A named part."] },
{ t: "Assessed product demonstration", o: "I can demonstrate my product and explain how its program works.",
  k: ["demonstrate: to show something working", "explain: to say how or why it works", "terminology: the correct technical words"],
  d: ["Find the line of your program that makes the main decision. Be ready to explain it.", "The student's own line."],
  s: ["The demonstration|Who is it for and what problem does it solve?|Show it working|Three minutes for each team",
      "Individual questions|Each person is asked about one part of the code|Use the words input, output, sensor, threshold, variable, loop|Say what would happen if a line were changed",
      "How you are assessed|The product meets its success criteria|The test table is complete|Your explanation of the code is accurate"],
  a: "Demonstrate your product as a team, then answer your individual question about the code and complete the written explanation. Support: use the explanation sentence starters. Stretch: explain how the device would need to change to be sold as a real product.",
  w: [["Who is your product for and what does it do?", "A clear user and purpose."], ["Describe the input, process and output.", "Three parts."], ["Explain the line of code that makes the main decision.", "An accurate explanation using the word condition or threshold."], ["What is the `while True:` loop for in your program?", "It keeps the device checking its inputs for as long as it is switched on."], ["What would happen if your threshold were much lower?", "A reasoned prediction."]],
  x: ["What question were you asked, and how well did you answer it?", "A self-assessment."] },
{ t: "Evaluation, feedback and reflection", o: "I can evaluate a product and reflect on working in a team.",
  k: ["evaluate: to judge how well something meets its aims, with evidence", "teamwork: working with others towards a shared goal", "improvement: a change that would make the product better"],
  d: ["What is the difference between 'It worked well' and an evaluation?", "An evaluation judges against the success criteria and gives evidence."],
  s: ["Evaluate the product|Take each success criterion in turn|Met, partly met or not met, with test evidence|Would the user really use it?",
      "Evaluate the teamwork|What did each role contribute?|What would you do differently as a team?|What did you learn from a teammate?",
      "The year in review|Python loops, networks, web pages, data, logic, physical computing|Which ideas appeared in more than one unit?|Next year: lists and functions, algorithms, security, the CPU"],
  a: "Write an evaluation of your product against each success criterion with evidence, two improvements, and a reflection on your team role. Then complete the end-of-year reflection. Support: use the evaluation frame. Stretch: redesign one part of the product on paper to show an improvement.",
  w: [["Which success criteria were fully met? Give evidence for one.", "A list with test evidence."], ["State two specific improvements.", "Two improvements with reasons."], ["What did you contribute in your role?", "A specific contribution."], ["What would your team do differently next time?", "A specific change."], ["Which unit this year did you find most useful, and why?", "A reasoned choice."]],
  x: ["In one sentence, how can a program sense and change the real world?", "It reads values from sensors, compares them with thresholds to make decisions, and controls outputs such as lights, sound and motors."] }
] }); })(typeof module !== "undefined" ? require("../lessons.js") : window.Toolkit);
